  "name": "24 HR URINARY HOMOVANILLIC ACID (HVA)",
  "code": "1116",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"137\" data-end=\"395\"><strong data-start=\"137\" data-end=\"164\">Homovanillic Acid (HVA)</strong> is the primary <strong data-start=\"180\" data-end=\"206\">metabolite of dopamine</strong>, a catecholamine neurotransmitter. Measuring <strong data-start=\"252\" data-end=\"275\">24-hour urinary HVA</strong> helps assess <strong data-start=\"289\" data-end=\"312\">dopamine metabolism</strong> and is especially useful in evaluating <strong data-start=\"352\" data-end=\"386\">catecholamine-secreting tumors</strong> such as:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"397\" data-end=\"481\">\n<li data-start=\"397\" data-end=\"437\">\n<p data-start=\"399\" data-end=\"437\"><strong data-start=\"399\" data-end=\"416\">Neuroblastoma</strong> (common in children)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"438\" data-end=\"458\">\n<p data-start=\"440\" data-end=\"458\"><strong data-start=\"440\" data-end=\"458\">Ganglioneuroma</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"459\" data-end=\"481\">\n<p data-start=\"461\" data-end=\"481\"><strong data-start=\"461\" data-end=\"481\">Pheochromocytoma</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"483\" data-end=\"627\">This test is typically part of the diagnostic workup for <strong data-start=\"540\" data-end=\"575\">suspected neuroendocrine tumors</strong> and can help in monitoring treatment or recurrence.</p>\n<p data-start=\"483\" data-end=\"627\">&nbsp;</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Elevated HVA May Indicate</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Neuroblastoma</strong> &ndash; Especially in pediatric cases; often accompanied by elevated VMA.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ganglioneuroblastoma or Ganglioneuroma</strong> &ndash; Other neural crest tumors.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pheochromocytoma</strong> &ndash; Although more commonly linked to elevated VMA/metanephrines.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Use of dopamine agonists or levodopa</strong> &ndash; Can artificially elevate HVA levels.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Stress, exercise, or certain foods</strong> &ndash; May cause transient increases.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p data-start=\"483\" data-end=\"627\">&nbsp;</p>",
